---
import Feature from "../../layouts/Feature.astro";
import Shot from "../../components/Shot.astro";
import audioL from "../../assets/shots/editor-audio-light.webp";
import audioD from "../../assets/shots/editor-audio-dark.webp";
import capL from "../../assets/shots/editor-captions-light.webp";
import capD from "../../assets/shots/editor-captions-dark.webp";
import CaptionStyles from "../../components/CaptionStyles.astro";
---
<Feature
  seoTitle="Clean audio and on-device captions: Reelbird"
  description="Auto levels, voice enhancement and music ducking, plus captions from Apple's on-device speech recognition. No API keys, nothing leaves your Mac."
  path="/features/audio-captions/" og="features-audio-captions" name="Audio and captions"
  title="Clean audio and on-device captions"
  lede="People forgive a soft picture but not bad sound. Reelbird levels your voice, takes out room noise and ducks system audio while you speak. Captions are made on your Mac. Your original tracks are never changed."
>
  <section class="fsec wrap fgrid">
    <div>
      <h2>Your voice at a normal level</h2>
      <p>Reelbird measures your voice (ITU-R BS.1770 integrated loudness) and sets it to <strong>−16 LUFS</strong>, so a quiet take doesn't come out quiet. A boost slider sits on top if you want more. Every track and the final mix go through a look-ahead limiter with a <strong>−1.5 dBFS</strong> ceiling, so nothing clips.</p>
      <p>Microphone and system audio are recorded as separate tracks, so you can mute or lower system sound after the fact. Step by step: <a href="/guides/record-mac-screen-with-internal-audio/">record your Mac screen with internal audio</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <Shot src={audioL} dark={audioD} alt="The editor after Clean Up: cuts on the timeline, a 3× speed-up for typing, a bar reading 10 s shorter with Review and Undo, and the Audio inspector with Enhance voice, Auto level and the Clean Up button." sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 680px, 100vw" widths={[680, 1020, 1360, 2040, 2960]} />
  </section>

  <section class="fsec wrap">
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h2>Voice enhance</h2>
        <p>On Macs that have it, Apple's sound isolation (AUSoundIsolation) removes room noise and hum. Then a downward expander, a gentle compressor, a de-esser and a small presence lift. Pick light, medium, strong or max, and blend with the original. <strong>Hear original</strong> plays the raw track at matched loudness so you can compare.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Ducking</h2>
        <p>System audio dips about <strong>12 dB</strong> while you speak, starting just before each phrase and coming back over 600 ms, so music and app sounds never fight your voice. Speech is found by an energy detector on your voice track, on your Mac.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Clean Up</h2>
        <p>One button (<kbd>W</kbd>) tightens the whole take: long pauses get shorter instead of vanishing, ums and dead time at the start and end go, and silent typing or waiting speeds up. Cuts land in quiet spots with a soft join. <strong>Review</strong> lets you play, put back or apply each change; <strong>Undo</strong> puts everything back.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="fsec wrap fgrid flip">
    <div>
      <h2>Captions made on your Mac</h2>
      <p>Captions come from Apple's on-device speech recognition: SpeechAnalyzer on macOS 26 and later, which downloads its language model the first time, and on-device recognition on macOS 15. Both give word timings, saved next to the take.</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Pick the spoken language next to <strong>Generate Captions</strong>, from the languages Apple supports on your Mac.</li>
        <li>Six looks: Classic, Highlight (the spoken word lights up), Marker (a highlighter box follows your words), and Pop, One Word and Slam for social clips, in five colour sets and three sizes.</li>
        <li>Auto position puts captions under the video when there's room, and moves them to the top while you work near the bottom of the screen.</li>
        <li>Double-click a line to fix a word without breaking its timing, fix it everywhere, or hide a line. Share links also offer captions as a track viewers can turn on.</li>
        <li>No API keys and no cloud. Nothing leaves your Mac unless you share it.</li>
        <li>From the CLI, <code>reelbird suggest</code> writes a title and chapters with Apple's on-device model on Macs with Apple Intelligence.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <Shot src={capL} dark={capD} alt="The Captions inspector: English (US) with Change, a grid of caption styles with Highlight selected, colour, size and position controls, and the transcript; the preview shows a Highlight caption with the spoken word in yellow." sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 680px, 100vw" widths={[680, 1020, 1360, 2040, 2960]} />
  </section>
  <section class="fsec wrap">
    <h2>Six caption styles</h2>
    <p class="cs-intro">Pick a look in the Captions tab and it's drawn into the video. Classic, Highlight and Marker suit tutorials and demos; Pop, One Word and Slam are for short social clips. Each comes in five colour sets and three sizes.</p>
    <CaptionStyles />
  </section>
</Feature>

<style>
  .cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 2.5rem; }
  .cols h2 { font-size: var(--step-2) !important; }
  .cs-intro { max-width: var(--prose); color: var(--text-muted); }
  @media (max-width: 60rem) { .cols { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } }
</style>
